Z||000044||Voytek||05-31-2001||02:10 PM||||85blackGT: Czego nie rozumiesz?<BR>You say you've blown doors off MR2's with your dad's 2M4.  Was there some MR2's too close to your Fiero when your engine blew up?<BR>I'm exaggerating of'course, but these are my conclusions (some from my own street racing experience):<BR>1.  The early MR2 4-cyl was comparable to the Fiero 4-Tech.<BR>2.  The early supercharged MR2 was comparable to any Fiero GT.<BR>3.  The late model 4-cyl MR2 could take an older 2M4 BUT, will definitely be beaten by a Fiero V6.<BR>4.  The late model turbo MR2 will blow away any non modified Fiero.<P>And that's that.<P>FirstFiero: Americans used to make crap in the 80's (except the Fiero of'course  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ).  I've recently heard a business report on the radio (I'm not sure whom they were quoting) It said that the gap between Jap and American cars have bridged the quality gap to the Jap cars in the last 4 years.  I don't have a doubt that this is true.  On the other hand, you get a WHOLE LOTTA value for your money when you buy American.<BR>TOYOTA's are good cars (I've had 2 ealy 80's Tercels).  BUT, both of them had transmission problems.  A friend of mine had an earlier Celica.  Same thing, transmission.<BR>I won't even get into Eagle Talons (Mitsubishi Eclipse, I believe).  The early cars were just plain SH*T.  I think they were worse than Fieros with all their engine and tranny problems.  And Hondas?  Well, this is my experience: my neighbor had a Special Edition Prelude (about an '89).  For two winters in a row, when the temperatures got down to -20 and lower, his car would sit in front of the house for 2 weeks at a time until it got towed to a shop.  The thing was a piece of sh*t.  I used to laugh at him when I left for work in -40 temperatures and started my '85 2M4 without even pluggin it in.  I've also recently seen a new S2000 in a fairly minor accident.  The whole front/side was peeled like a tin can all the way to passenger side.  I guess the $50,000 CDN is for the engine, right?||||reg||1
Z||000045||firstfiero||06-01-2001||10:25 AM||||       All I can tell you is what I read. I know from my own experience with cars the only time when I have to take advantage of the warrenty is with american cars...My geo storm gsi was the biggest pos imaginable...every ford ranger I have has been back for one reason or another...My 2001 chevy blazer with only 8 grand in mileage blew a seal in the tranny.  Thing is I go threw alot of cars..my wifes parents buy us a new car about every 9 months to a year so rarely do I see high mileage on a car. My Ford zx2 was the only vehicle that didn't breack down. We have gotten better but for the most part were still making **** !<P>------------------<BR>Hey Mr. Ricer come over here and tell me how your stickers make you faster.....<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/headshot.gif">         <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||13
Z||000046||Fiero5||06-01-2001||01:08 PM||||1. The early MR2 4-cyl was comparable to the Fiero 4-Tech.<P>OK, no argument there.<P>2. The early supercharged MR2 was comparable to any Fiero GT.<P>Again, no argument. Though a good solid well cared for 4 banger is pretty damn quick too!<P>3. The late model 4-cyl MR2 could take an older 2M4 BUT, will definitely be beaten by a Fiero V6.<P>I disagree with the first part. It isn't always power that wins, but many times how you drive compared to another. Equally skilled drivers in each, that may be so. I have beaten a few turbocharged MR2's with my 4 bangers. I don't care about who has more HP or displacement. The fact remains that against that paerticular car and that particular driver I won. I am sure that it was more driver than car, heh, heh.<P>4. The late model turbo MR2 will blow away any non modified Fiero.<P>Yup, I have beaten a few, but I think the drivers in those cases may have been a bit gun shy or something. Probably working on his/her third speeding ticket or something, 'cause those babies are quick!<P>Steve<BR>------------------<P>The First 6 Kelley Fieros<P>------------------<BR>                         <IMG SRC="http://www.kelleyfamilyfieros.homestead.com/files/dads87GT.jpg">                       <BR>http://www.kelleyfamilyfieros.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||12
Z||000047||Voytek||06-01-2001||02:18 PM||||FirstFiero: A Geo Storm is an Isuzu made for GM, i.e. NOT AN AMERICAN CAR.<BR>So you've experienced some problems with GM's.  No doubt, they're not the best vehicles in the world (and definitely weren't in the 80's).  BUT, I had a friend with an Eagle Talon (Mitsubishi Eclipse).  It was a '92.  The guy went through 3 tannys and 2 engines by the time it was 5 years old and he was no more of an aggressive driver than I am with my Fiero (which, by the way, has about 125,000 miles on it (200,000 kms) and the tranny is still going strong).  Did you also know that the Honda Del Sol is one of the worst cars for safety on the road?  It was voted worst car for safety of the year (in '92 or '93 - I don't quite remember).  While I do like certain Japanese cars, I wouldn't generalize the way you do.  The press has always been biased against domestic vehicles.  Just read Car & Driver about the Fiero from year to year.  The first year the car is praised like it's the best thing since sliced bread (yes, including the suspension).  By '87 the car is portrayed as sh*t (even though now it has a V6 and is otherwise similar to the '84 - go figure!).  By '88 they barely admit that it's a decent car and still rate it as low as they can.  They don't even MENTION safety, dent resistant panels or independent suspension.  Biased?  Just slightly.  This is what they did to other American cars.||||reg||1
